{"cuisine/cuisine-of-omaha":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-omaha","n":"Cuisine of Omaha","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The cuisine of Omaha reflects the heritage and culture of Omaha, Nebraska."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-omaha"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Omaha"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/hopi-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/hopi-foodways","n":"Hopi foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Pueblo foodways","cuisine/pueblo-foodways"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Hopi mesas, northeastern Arizona","names":"Hopi","intro":[["On the mesas of northeastern Arizona, Hopi farmers grow corn, and ",["Blue corn","ingredient/blue-corn"]," is the base of ",["Piki","dish/piki"],", a paper-thin bread of cornmeal, ash and water spread by hand on a hot stone. Hopi women make it, a skill that takes years to learn, and a well kept piki stone passes from mother to daughter."],["Piki is everyday food, easy to carry to the fields, and visitors are often offered a fresh roll. It also marks weddings, baby namings and other ceremonies."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Penn Museum, Expedition magazine, Piki of the Hopi Indians","url":"https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/piki-of-the-hopi-indians/"},{"name":"Crow Canyon Archaeological Center, Pueblo food today","url":"https://crowcanyon.org/EducationProducts/pueblo_history_kids/modern_food.php"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"hopi-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Piki","dish/piki"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Pueblo foodways","cuisine/pueblo-foodways"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Blue corn","ingredient/blue-corn"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Pueblo foodways","cuisine/pueblo-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/48/Piki_bread_at_La_Posada%2C_Santa_Fe.jpg/500px-Piki_bread_at_La_Posada%2C_Santa_Fe.jpg","w":500,"h":373,"by":"Alan Levine from Strawberry, United States","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piki_bread_at_La_Posada%2C_Santa_Fe.jpg","of":"Piki"}},"dish/badambura":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/badambura","n":"Badambura","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Azerbaijani cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Badambura is an Azerbaijani sweet pastry, usually eaten as a dessert, especially when celebrating Nowruz."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-badambura"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Badambura"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Azerbaijani cuisine","cuisine/azerbaijani-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"]]},{"h":"More from Azerbaijani cuisine","links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Doghramaj","dish/doghramaj"],["Dovga","dish/dovga"],["Eggplant jam","dish/eggplant-jam"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Shekerbura","dish/shekerbura"],["Sheki halva","dish/sheki-halva"],["Shorgoghal","dish/shorgoghal"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/8/8a/Badambura_Novruz_%C5%9Firniyyat%C4%B1.jpg/500px-Badambura_Novruz_%C5%9Firniyyat%C4%B1.jpg","w":500,"h":705,"by":"Sefer azeri","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badambura_Novruz_%C5%9Firniyyat%C4%B1.jpg"}},"ingredient/calcium-oxide":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/calcium-oxide","n":"Calcium oxide","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Calcium oxide, commonly known as quicklime or burnt lime, is a widely used chemical compound. It is a white, caustic, alkaline, crystalline solid at room temperature. The broadly used term lime connotes calcium-containing inorganic compounds, in which carbonates, oxides, and hydroxides of calcium, silicon, magnesium, aluminium, and iron predominate. By contrast, quicklime specifically applies to the single compound calcium oxide. Calcium oxide that survives processing without reacting in building products, such as cement, is called free lime."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-calcium-oxide"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calcium_oxide"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Century egg","dish/century-egg"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Clay","ingredient/clay"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Rice hulls","ingredient/rice-hulls"],["Wood ash","ingredient/wood-ash"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/0/09/Calcium-oxide-3D-vdW.png/500px-Calcium-oxide-3D-vdW.png","w":500,"h":475,"by":"Benjah-bmm27","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calcium-oxide-3D-vdW.png"}},"dish/croffle":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/croffle","n":"Croffle","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["The croffle, croiffle or crofle is a hybrid of a croissant and waffle. It was popularized in South Korea and then spread throughout the world. The concept is reminiscent of another pastry, the Cronut, which is a croissant and donut hybrid  created by Dominique Ansel."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-croffle"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croffle"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"],["Irish cuisine","cuisine/irish-cuisine"],["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Croissant","ingredient/croissant"],["Waffle","ingredient/waffle"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Irish American Mom","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"irishamericanmom.com","note":"Irish-born cook living in the US sharing traditional Irish recipes.","url":"https://www.irishamericanmom.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/d/d9/Croffle_1.jpg/500px-Croffle_1.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Scudsvlad","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Croffle_1.jpg"}},"ingredient/alcoholic-beverage":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/alcoholic-beverage","n":"Alcoholic beverage","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["An alcoholic beverage (also called an alcoholic drink or strong drink) is any drink that contains alcohol. Alcoholic beverages are typically divided into three classes: beers, wines, and spirits; with alcohol content typically between 3% and 50%. The exact amount on which a beverage is considered alcoholic differs by country, with some considering drinks containing less than 0.5% to be non-alcoholic. These beverages are primarily consumed for the psychoactive effects that they produce."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-alcoholic-beverage"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcoholic_beverage"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Drunken shrimp","dish/drunken-shrimp"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/a/a8/Common_alcoholic_beverages.jpg/500px-Common_alcoholic_beverages.jpg","w":500,"h":335,"by":"TrafficJan82","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Common_alcoholic_beverages.jpg"}},"ingredient/domestic-duck":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/domestic-duck","n":"Domestic duck","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Domestic ducks (mainly mallards, Anas platyrhynchos domesticus, with some Muscovy ducks, Cairina moschata domestica) are ducks that have been domesticated and raised for meat and eggs. A few are kept for show,  or for their ornamental value. Domesticated ducks are descended mainly from the mallard, which was domesticated in China around 2000 BC, with contributions from the Muscovy duck."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-domestic-duck"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domestic_duck"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Domestic goose","ingredient/domestic-goose"],["Liver","ingredient/liver"],["Liver as food","ingredient/liver-as-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/b2/Taiwanese_duck_farm.jpg/500px-Taiwanese_duck_farm.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"MiNe (sfmine79) from Taipei, Taiwan","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taiwanese_duck_farm.jpg"}},"ingredient/fried-rice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/fried-rice","n":"Fried rice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Fried rice is a dish of cooked rice that has been stir-fried in a wok or a frying pan and is usually mixed with other ingredients such as eggs, vegetables, seafood, or meat. It is often eaten as a main dish or as an accompaniment to another dish. Fried rice is a popular component of East Asian, Southeast Asian, South Asian and American Chinese cuisines. As a homemade dish, fried rice is typically made with ingredients left over from other dishes, leading to countless variations. Fried rice first developed during the Sui dynasty in China and later spread across the world through the migration of the Chinese diaspora."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-fried-rice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fried_rice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Javanese fried rice","dish/javanese-fried-rice"],["Nasi goreng","dish/nasi-goreng"]]],["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine",[["Nasi goreng","dish/nasi-goreng"]]],["Singaporean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine-chinese-inspired",[["Nasi goreng","dish/nasi-goreng"]]],["Singaporean cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine",[["Nasi goreng","dish/nasi-goreng"]]],["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku",[["Volga rice","dish/volga-rice"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Sweet soy sauce","ingredient/sweet-soy-sauce"],["Breaded cutlet","ingredient/breaded-cutlet"],["Omelette","ingredient/omelette"],["Omurice","ingredient/omurice"],["Tonkatsu","ingredient/tonkatsu"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/c/c3/Koh_Mak%2C_Thailand%2C_Fried_rice_with_seafood%2C_Thai_fried_rice.jpg/500px-Koh_Mak%2C_Thailand%2C_Fried_rice_with_seafood%2C_Thai_fried_rice.jpg","w":500,"h":350,"by":"© Vyacheslav Argenberg","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koh_Mak%2C_Thailand%2C_Fried_rice_with_seafood%2C_Thai_fried_rice.jpg"}},"dish/jokai-bean-soup":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jokai-bean-soup","n":"Jókai bean soup","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Hungarian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Jókai bean soup (Jókai bableves) is a Hungarian bean soup. It  is a rich bean soup with many vegetables, smoked pork hock pieces and noodles. It is often made to be spicy or some sort of hot chili offered with it. Despite its richness it is served with sour cream on top and white bread, and this soup is a lunch or dinner in itself."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jokai-bean-soup"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J%C3%B3kai_bean_soup"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hungarian cuisine","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Zsuzsa Is in the Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"zsuzsaisinthekitchen.blogspot.com","note":"Hungarian-born home cook's blog of traditional Hungarian recipes.","url":"https://zsuzsaisinthekitchen.blogspot.com/2010/01","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Hungarian cuisine","links":[["Arany galuska","dish/arany-galuska"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chicken paprikash","dish/chicken-paprikash"],["Esterházy torte","dish/esterhazy-torte"],["Goulash","dish/goulash"],["Gulyásleves","dish/gulyasleves"],["Kürtőskalács","dish/kurtoskalacs"],["Lecsó","dish/lecso"],["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"],["Palóc soup","dish/paloc-soup"],["Pörkölt","dish/porkolt"],["Rakott krumpli","dish/rakott-krumpli"],["Rigójancsi","dish/rigojancsi"],["Sour cherry soup","dish/sour-cherry-soup"],["Speckkuchen","dish/speckkuchen"],["Székelykáposzta","dish/szekelykaposzta"],["Tarhonya","dish/tarhonya"],["Trdelník","dish/trdelnik"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/e/e0/Pasulj.jpg","w":500,"h":401,"by":"User:Djzare","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pasulj.jpg"}},"ingredient/phoenix-dactylifera":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/phoenix-dactylifera","n":"Phoenix dactylifera","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Phoenix dactylifera, commonly known as the date palm, is a flowering-plant species in the palm family Arecaceae, native to the region from the Gulf States and Iraq to Pakistan and India. It is cultivated for its edible sweet fruit called dates. The species is widely cultivated across northern Africa, the Middle East, Australia, South and Southeast Asia, the coastal Mediterranean basin, and Southern California. It is naturalised in many tropical and subtropical regions worldwide. P. dactylifera is the type species of genus Phoenix, which contains 12–19 species of wild date palms."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-phoenix-dactylifera"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phoenix_dactylifera"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Arab cuisine","cuisine/arab-cuisine",[["Ma'amoul","dish/ma-amoul"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Pistachios","ingredient/pistachios"],["Walnuts","ingredient/walnuts"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/45/Dates005.jpg/500px-Dates005.jpg","w":500,"h":335,"by":"Nepenthes","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dates005.jpg"}},"dish/mokthuk":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mokthuk","n":"Mokthuk","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Mokthuk or Mothuk is a type of momo soup. It is popular in Tibet, Nepal and Ladakh (a region in northern India). It originates from Tibet. It is a combination of momo and thukpa where unlike the momos, the shape of the dumplings are usually smaller known as tsi-tsi momos."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mokthuk"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mokthuk"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian